# Chapter 7, part 4: your own words and Setup.
.h2 Maintain Dictionaries [str-spell-words]
.ix Maintain Dictionaries
.ix Dictionaries, maintaining
.ix Word list, personal
.ix Words of your own
.ix Define, adding a word
Maintain Dictionaries is the entry for the words of your own, the words that
the spell check must not ask about.  The words that every language has come
from the checking program of the machine ({c:aspell} or {c:hunspell}); your own
words
are a list that you keep.  One list serves every language of the spell check.
Define on the check screen of Tools, Spell ({n:spell-dictionaries}) adds a
word to the list at once, and Maintain Dictionaries shows the list and lets you
change it.  The spell check reads the list at each start, so a change is seen
at once, also while the Editor is running.  The same panel has a second
list, your hyphenation list ({n:str-spell-words-hyphen}), which says where
words may be broken at the end of a line in the print; {k:Tab} goes from one
list to the other.
.h3 The panel [str-spell-words-panel]
.ix Maintain Dictionaries, panel
The panel shows your words in order, without regard to the case of the letters,
and shows at the top how many there are.  The cursor is on one word.  A list
that is longer than the panel scrolls, with an arrow at the edge.  The keys are
on the foot line.
.table 10 50
Key | What it does
{k:Tab} | Shows the other list: the spelling words or the hyphenation list.
 | The first line shows which list it is, its number of words and the key.
{k:Ins} | Adds a word.  A box prompts for it; {k:Enter} adds it.
{k:Del} | Takes out the word that has the cursor, after the question Delete
 | WORD from your words?
{k:F3} | Imports the words of a text file ({n:str-spell-words-import}).
{k:F4} | Exports the list to a text file ({n:str-spell-words-import}).
Letters | Move the cursor to the first word that begins with the letters typed,
 | without regard to case.  The letters are shown on the foot line as Find.
 | {k:Backspace} takes the last letter back.
{k:Up} {k:Down} | Move the cursor one word.
{k:PgUp} {k:PgDn} | Move it a page.
{k:Home} {k:End} | Move it to the first or the last word.
{k:F1} | Shows the help of the panel.
{k:Esc} | Leaves the panel for the menu.
.end
.pic pictures/str-dict-spelling.png 5 The panel of Maintain Dictionaries: your own words for the spell check.
Every change is saved when it is made; there is nothing to accept.  Each change
is made on the list as it is at that moment, so a word that Define added while
the panel was open is not lost.
.h3 What is a word [str-spell-words-rules]
.ix Words, what the list accepts
A word has letters, apostrophes and hyphens, begins and ends with a letter,
has no space and no digit, and is at most 65 characters long.  Accented
letters are letters.  The list does not keep a word twice.  A word in lower
case also stands for the same word with a capital first, so adding Garden when
garden is in the list is not needed and shows that it is there already; any
other entry is only that word.  Adding garden when only Garden is in the list
is allowed, because it is a different word.
.h3 The hyphenation list [str-spell-words-hyphen]
.ix Hyphenation list
.ix Words, where they may be broken
.ix Hyphenation, your own words
.ix Never break a word
TeX breaks the words of a paragraph with patterns for English
({n:print-hyphenation}) and breaks some words in the wrong place, and others
(names, terms of a trade) in places that the rules do not cover.  The hyphenation list
is the place to say it once.  Press {k:Tab} in the panel and the list of words with their
places is shown; the keys are the same as for the spelling words.  The
list is the file {c:hyphenation} of the settings directory, and the print reads
it each time.  A change is in the next print.
A word is written with a hyphen at each place where it may be broken:
{c:hy-phen-a-tion}.  The word is then broken at those places and
nowhere else.  A word with no hyphen is never broken; an equals sign in
front ({c:=Jacksonville}) says so in so many words.  The case of the letters
does not matter, and a word with a capital first is broken as the same word
in lower case.  A word that the list has, with the same letters and other
places, is the same word: the list keeps the first (delete it first to
change the places).
.table 13 47
Rule | What it means
Letters | Letters of the PC character set, accented ones too.  No digit, no
 | apostrophe and no space: TeX cannot break such a word.
Hyphens | Only between two letters, and not two in a row.
Equals sign | Only in front, and then no hyphen follows: the word is never broken.
Length | At most 65 characters.
Order | The words are in order of their letters, without the hyphens.
Find | Letters typed in the panel find a word through its hyphens.
.end
.pic pictures/str-dict-hyphenation.png 5 The hyphenation list: a hyphen where a word may be broken, an equals sign for a word that is never broken.
The place where the file is read is the settings directory.  A line of the file that is not an entry is not shown
and not used.  The print gives the words to TeX in front of the document;
a word that has a character that the fonts of the print do not have is left
out.  A soft hyphen typed in the text ({n:edit-shy}) is for one place in one document; the
list is for every document.
.h3 Import and export [str-spell-words-import]
.ix Words, import
.ix Words, export
.ix Maintain Dictionaries, import and export
{k:F3} prompts for the name of a text file with one word on a line and adds the
words that the list does not have.  The text is read as UTF-8; a file that is
not UTF-8 at all (a character that no UTF-8 text explains) is read in the PC
character set, as old word lists are, so that the words with
accents keep their letters.  A line that is
not a word, or is longer than a word can be, is left out.  In the hyphenation
list {k:F3} reads a text file with one word on a line, with its hyphens, in the same way, or a
hyphenation dictionary in the old binary form: a file whose name ends in
{c:.hyc} (any case) gives its words with the places at which each may be
broken (a word with none is never broken); a word with a digit or an
apostrophe is counted as not a word.  A box then shows how many words were
added, how many
the list had already (a word twice in the file counts as had already), and how
many lines were not words.  Nothing is added when the file cannot be read.
{k:F4} prompts for a name and writes the list that is shown to that file, one word on a line, in
order.  A file of that name that is there is replaced only after the question
NAME exists.  Replace it?  The list itself is not changed.
Both panels ask for a Working Directory and a File Name.  A name that begins
with a slash is taken as it is; any other name is in the working directory.
{k:F2} or {k:Keypad +} in a field lists the directories or the files.
.h3 Messages of Maintain Dictionaries [str-spell-words-messages]
.ix Maintain Dictionaries, messages
.table 31 29
Text | Where and why
Type a word | The box of Ins was accepted with no word.
A word has no spaces | The word has a space.
A word has no digits | The word has a digit.
A word has letters, apostrophes and hyphens only | The word has another
 | character.
A word begins and ends with a letter | The word begins or ends with an
 | apostrophe or a hyphen.
A word has at most 65 letters | The word is longer than that.
A word has letters and hyphens only | Hyphenation list: the word has another
 | character.
TeX cannot break a word with a digit or an apostrophe | Hyphenation list.
A hyphen needs a letter on both sides | Hyphenation list: two hyphens in a row.
A word with an equals sign has no hyphen | Hyphenation list: =word with a place.
Type the word after the equals sign | Hyphenation list: only the sign.
WORD is in the list already | The list has the word or stands for it.
Added WORD | The word was added.
Deleted WORD | The word was taken out.
Imported: N added, N in the list already, N not words | An import is done.
Wrote N words to NAME | An export is done.
Enter the name of the file | The box of an import or an export was accepted
 | with no name.
Cannot read NAME: REASON | The file of an import cannot be read.
Cannot write NAME: REASON | The list or an export cannot be written.
.end

.h2 Compare Document Versions [str-compare]
.ix Compare Document Versions
.ix Documents, comparing
.ix Differences between documents
.ix Versions of a document
.ix Backup file, comparing
Compare Document Versions shows what is different between two documents, such
as a document and the backup file that was kept when it was saved.  The report
lists the blocks that were inserted, removed, changed or moved, the sections
that were added, removed, moved or put on another level, and the blocks whose
words are the same but whose format, markers or attributes are not.  A block
is a paragraph, a headline or a table.
.h3 The panel [str-compare-panel]
.ix Compare Document Versions, panel
.table 20 40
Field | What it does
Working Directory | The directory that the names of the documents are
 | taken from.  It begins with the working directory of the Document
 | Manager.  {k:F2} lists the directories.
First Document | The first document.  It begins with the document that was
 | used last.  {k:F2} lists the documents of the working directory.  A
 | name without a dot gets {c:.doc}; a name that begins with a slash is a
 | full name.
Second Document | The second document.  When the first document has a backup
 | file ({c:.backup/name.bak01}, the newest backup), the backup file is
 | offered here.
Report Document | The name that the report is saved under, when it is
 | saved.  It begins as the name of the first document with {c:-diff}
 | added, in the same directory, when a document was used last.  Left empty,
 | the name is made from the First Document in the same way.
.end
.pic pictures/str-compare-panel.png 5 The panel of Compare Document Versions.
{k:Ins} compares the two documents.  The panel does not accept the same
document twice, a document that cannot be found, or a report name that is the
name of one of the two documents (the report never replaces them).  When the
comparison is done the report is shown, and afterwards the panel is shown
again, so that another pair can be compared.  {k:Esc} leaves the panel.
.h3 The report [str-compare-report]
.ix Comparison report
.ix Report, comparison
The report is shown in a box that fills the screen, with the title Comparison
of two documents and the number of the line at the top right.
.pic pictures/str-compare-report.png 5 The report of two versions of a memo: four blocks changed.
.table 20 40
Key | What it does
{k:Up} {k:Down} | One line.
{k:PgUp} {k:PgDn} | One screen.
{k:Esc} {k:Enter} | Closes the report.
.end
The report has these parts.
.table 10 50
Part | What it holds
The head | The names of the two documents and how many blocks and sections
 | each has.
Summary | How many blocks were inserted, removed, changed and moved, how
 | many have another format, and how many sections were added,
 | removed, moved or put on another level.
Sections | One line for each section that was added, removed, moved or
 | put on another level, with its number and its headline.  A section is
 | named by its outline number, such as 2.1.
Changes | One entry for each block.  Its first line shows what happened, the
 | number of the block in each document and the section that it is in;
 | the lines after it show the words.
.end
The entries of Changes are of these kinds.
.table 20 40
Entry | Meaning
Inserted | A block that is only in the second document.  Its words are
 | shown between [+ and +].
Removed | A block that is only in the first document.  Its words are shown
 | between [- and -].
Changed | A block in both documents with other words.  The words that were
 | removed are between [- and -] and the words that were added are
 | between [+ and +]; the other words are as they are.
Moved | A block that has the same words in both documents but is in
 | another place.
Format changed | The words of the block are the same, but its format is
 | not.
Markers or attributes changed | The words are the same, but a marker, or
 | the bold, italic or underline of some of the words, is not.
.end
When the two documents have the same text, the same sections and the same
format, the report says so and nothing is offered to save.
.h3 Saving the report [str-compare-save]
.ix Report, saving
When the report is closed a prompt reads "Save the comparison as a document?".
Yes writes the report to the Report Document as an ordinary
document, which the Editor opens like any other: the words that were added are
bold in it and the words that were removed are underlined, and the marks [+ +]
and [- -] are in the text, so that a printed copy can be read without color or
type.  When the file exists, a prompt reads "NAME exists.  Replace it?"; No
leaves the file as it is.  The notice "Wrote NAME" says that the
report was written.
.h3 What is compared [str-compare-rules]
.ix Compare Document Versions, rules
- The words of each block are compared, without the markers, the format and the
  attributes.  Table columns are compared as one text.  The space at the
  start and the end of a block does not count.
- A block that was removed and a block that was inserted at the same place are
  one block that changed when at least four tenths of their words are the same.
  Otherwise they are listed as removed and inserted.
- A block that is removed in one place and inserted in another place with the
  same words is listed as moved.  A moved headline is a section that moved.
- The format, the markers and the attributes of two blocks with the same words
  are compared, and a difference is listed on its own.
- A document with no sections has blocks only.  The text before the first
  section of a structured document is not a headline.
- When more than three thousand blocks differ in one stretch of the two
  documents, or more than fifteen hundred words of one block, the detail is
  not worked out: the stretch is listed as blocks removed and inserted, and the
  summary says that part of the documents is too different for a detailed
  comparison.
.h3 Messages of Compare Document Versions [str-compare-messages]
.ix Compare Document Versions, messages
.table 31 29
Text | Where and why
Enter the names of the two documents | The panel was accepted with a name
 | missing.
Cannot find NAME | One of the two documents does not exist.
Choose two different documents | The two names are the same document.
The report must not replace one of the two documents | The Report Document
 | is one of the two documents.
Cannot read NAME: REASON | A document could not be read (it is not a
 | Strata document, or the file cannot be opened).
NAME exists.  Replace it? | The report is saved and the file exists.
Cannot write NAME: REASON | The report could not be written.
Wrote NAME | The report was written.
.end

.h2 Restore Backup [str-restore]
.ix Restore Backup
.ix Backup, restoring
.ix Documents, restoring a backup
.ix Swap of a document and a backup
Restore Backup brings back one of the backups of a document ({n:file-backup}).
The panel prompts for the {b:Working Directory} and the {b:Document Name}
({k:F2} lists the documents; a name without a dot gets {c:.doc}).  {k:Ins}
shows a list of the backups of that document, newest first.  A line has the
number, the date and time of the file, its size and the first words of the
document, such as {c:01  02 Jan 2026 10:20  1893 bytes  Orchard Plan}; the
word {c:old} after the number marks a backup that an earlier version left
beside the document instead of in the directory {c:.backup}.  A special
backup, the copy that the Editor made of a file of an older version of the
file format when it loaded the file ({c:V2-memo.doc} for {c:memo.doc}, see
{n:file-backup-special}), is listed after the numbered backups with {c:V} and
its version, such as {c:V2}; the menu of the backup and the pager are titled
{c:Special backup V2}.  A special backup is never removed.
.pic pictures/str-restore-panel.png 5 The panel of Restore Backup.
.pic pictures/str-restore-list.png 5 The backups of a memo, newest first.
Choose a backup with {k:Enter} and a small menu offers {b:View the text}, which
shows the text of the backup in a pager ({k:Esc} comes back), {b:Restore this
backup} and {b:Back}.  The restore puts a question first, in a box that names the backup,
its date and the document and reports that the present document becomes backup 1;
{k:Y} restores and {k:N} or {k:Esc} leaves everything as it was.  The restore is
a swap, described in {x:file-backup-use}.  {k:Esc} on the list returns to the
panel and {k:Esc} on the panel to the menu.
.pic pictures/str-restore-menu.png 5 The small menu of a backup.
.pic pictures/str-restore-box.png 5 The question before a backup is restored.
.h3 Messages of Restore Backup [str-restore-messages]
.ix Restore Backup, messages
.table 26 34
Message | Meaning
Enter the name of a document | The Document Name of the panel is empty.
 | Type a name, or choose one with {k:F2}.
NAME has no backup | The document has no backup in the directory .backup and
 | none beside it.
The backup has no text to show | View the text, on a backup that holds no
 | text.  {k:Esc} closes the message.
Backup NN holds the same text as NAME | The backup and the document are the same;
 | nothing was done.
Restored backup NN over NAME. | The swap is made; the document that was there is
 | backup 1.
Special backup V2 holds the same text as NAME | The special backup and the
 | document are the same; nothing was done.
Restored special backup V2 over NAME. | The swap is made with a special backup;
 | the document that was there is backup 1 and the special backup stays.
cannot restore: ... | The restore was not made (a file named .backup is in the
 | way, the directory cannot be written, a step failed); the
 | text says why and the document and the backups are as
 | they were, or the text names the file that holds the old
 | document.
.end
.h2 Setup [str-setup]
.ix Setup
.ix Setup panel
.ix Settings, Document Manager
.ix Settings saved
.ix Panel, Setup
Setup is the panel of the settings of the user: the working directory, the
number of backups that Save keeps, the autosave, where a print goes and the
program that shows a PDF, the paper of a new document, the language of the
dictionary, the number of operations that Undo keeps, the height of the
windows.  Setup is one panel of three pages, as the panels of the Editor
that have more than one page are: the settings, the colors of the screen
and the settings that are seldom changed (the macro set and the library that
the Editor loads at start, the characters of the terminal, the Editor
program and the help file).  {k:PgUp} goes to the next page and {k:PgDn} to
the one before; after the last page {k:PgUp} returns to the first.  The
values are kept in the
file {c:~/.strata/config} (see
{n:appcfg}; the environment variable {c:STRATA_HOME} names another directory).
.pic pictures/str-setup-panel.png 5 The Setup panel.
.h3 The fields of Setup [str-setup-fields]
.ix Setup, fields
.ix Initial Working Directory
.ix Print To
.ix Printer Queue
.ix PDF Directory
.ix PDF Viewer
.ix Editor program
.ix Help File
.ix Autosave
.ix Autosave Interval (Seconds)
.ix Paper for New Documents
.ix Setup, Advanced
.ix Advanced, Setup
.ix Default, Setup
.ix Window Rows
.ix Dictionary Language
.ix Screen Colors
.ix Terminal Characters
The focus is on the first field when the panel opens.  The fields of the
first page stand in four groups, each under a heading: Documents, Printing,
Writing and Screen.  The title of the panel names the page: Setup (settings),
Setup (screen colors) or Setup (advanced settings), and the frame at the
bottom says {c:PgUp/PgDn: Page}.  A line at the foot
of the panel gives a few words about the field that has the focus.  At the right
of a field its default is shown, when there is room; {k:Del} puts the default
back.  A value is checked when you leave the field with {k:Up}, {k:Down} or
{k:Tab}: a wrong value keeps the focus in the field and the reason is shown in
the line at the foot in place of the words about the field.  Nothing that you
typed in another field is lost.
The panel is as high as a screen of 25 rows, with the line of the foot; in a
window of 20 rows the focus moves down and the panel scrolls (an arrow at the
edge says that there are more fields).
.pic pictures/str-setup-printto.png 4.4 Print To: F2 shows the list of the choices, File only and PDF viewer when the system has no print queue.
.pic pictures/str-setup-viewer.png 4.4 A PDF Viewer that is only a name is refused: the full path is needed, and the panel stays open.
.pic pictures/str-setup-scrolled.png 4.4 The Setup panel in a window of 20 rows: an arrow at the right edge says that there are more fields.
.table 24 36
Field | Value shown
Initial Working Directory | The directory of the panel
 | Edit / Preview / Print Document.
Backups Kept (0 to 99) | 3 by default.
Autosave | Yes or No: Yes by default.
Autosave Interval (Seconds) | 120 by default.
Print To | Printer, File only or PDF viewer: File only at the start.
Printer Queue | System default, or the saved queue.
PDF Directory | Empty, or the saved directory.
PDF Viewer | Empty (a viewer of {c:/usr/bin} is used), or the saved full path.
Paper for New Documents | Letter or A4: Letter by default.
Dictionary Language | en_US, or the saved code.
Undo Levels (0 to 500) | 100 by default.
Window Rows | Two fields on one line, X11 and Terminal: empty, or the
 | saved number of rows.
.end
The second page is the colors of the screen ({n:str-colours}).  The third
page, the advanced settings, has these fields:
.table 24 36
Field | Value shown
Initial Macro Set | Empty, or the saved name of a macro file.
Initial Library | Empty, or the saved name of a library file.
Terminal Characters | Automatic, ASCII, VT100 or UTF-8: VT100 at the start.
Editor program | The Editor that was found, or the saved path.
Help File | The help file that was found, or empty.
.end
Two lines of text under the fields tell where the templates are looked for and
where the settings are kept.  {k:Ins} on any page keeps what was typed on all
the pages and saves it; {k:Esc} on any page leaves every page as it was.
A wrong value on a page keeps the focus there: {k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn} do not
leave the page until the value is right.
.pic pictures/str-setup-advanced.png 5 The third page of Setup: the settings that are seldom changed.
.rows 26 44
Initial Macro Set | The macro set that the Editor loads at start, as
 | Tools, Macros, Retrieve with the option Overwrite Current Macros does, before
 | the first key is read.  Empty means none.  A name that begins with a slash
 | is the file itself; any other name is looked for in the working directory of
 | the Editor and then in the settings directory ({c:~/.strata}); a name with
 | no period gets {c:.mac}.  {k:Keypad +} or {k:F2} lists the files.  The name
 | is not checked when Setup is accepted: a file that is missing, cannot be
 | read or is not a macro set gives the message {c:Macro set not loaded:}
 | and the reason on the message line of the Editor and a line in the log, and
 | the Editor starts without it.  What is loaded at the start is not a change
 | of the document and makes no Undo operation.  The setting is one for the
 | terminal and the window version.  Saved as {c:initial_macros}; empty is
 | saved as nothing.  Learn and Save ask for a file name as they always did;
 | they do not write the file that was loaded.
Initial Library | The library that the Editor loads at start, as
 | Tools, Library, Retrieve with Overwrite does.  The name is found as for
 | the macro set, with {c:.lib} added to a name with no period.  A file that
 | cannot be used gives {c:Library not loaded:} and the reason, and the Editor
 | starts without it.  The library is not marked as changed until you change
 | it; Library Cut, Copy and Paste ({k:Ctrl-F3}, {k:Ctrl-F4}, {k:Ctrl-F5}) work
 | on it as on one that you retrieved, and Save writes to the name that you
 | give.  Saved as {c:initial_library}.
Paper for New Documents | A choice, Letter or A4 ({k:Space} takes the next).  The paper of a document that is made new and does not come from a
 | template, in the Document Manager or in the Editor: the Paper Size of
 | Global, Page is the one that was chosen.  A template keeps its own paper,
 | and a document that exists keeps its paper.  Letter, the default, is saved
 | as nothing; A4 is saved as {c:default_paper=a4}.
Dictionary Language | The language of the spell check, as a code such as
 | en_US or de_DE: letters, digits and the characters underscore, minus and
 | at sign.  Saved as {c:spell_language}; the default en_US is saved as
 | nothing.  The dictionary of that language must be installed for
 | {c:aspell} or {c:hunspell} ({n:spell}).
Initial Working Directory | The directory in which the panel
 | Edit / Preview / Print Document begins.  Empty is allowed and means the
 | current
 | directory of the start.  A directory that does not exist is refused.
 | {k:Keypad +} lists directories only; the first line of the list,
 | [Use this directory], takes the directory shown.  Saved as {c:workdir}.
Print To | Where a print goes when you do not say otherwise in the Print
 | panel: Printer (the PDF is made and sent to a print queue), File only
 | (the PDF is made and nothing is sent), or PDF viewer (the PDF is made
 | and opened in the PDF Viewer, to be printed from its own dialog,
 | {n:print-sending}).  It is a choice, as in the panels of the Editor:
 | {k:Right} takes the next entry (nothing happens at the last), {k:Left}
 | the one before (nothing at the first), {k:Space} the next, round to the
 | first, and {k:F2} or {k:Keypad +} shows the list; a letter does nothing.
 | The foot of the screen names these keys.  An older settings file that has
 | Open to print or open is read as PDF viewer, and is saved as {c:viewer}.
 | The panels of the Print menu, and a print that is made without a panel,
 | start with it.  When the system has no print queue, Printer is
 | not offered; a saved Printer is kept, shown as not available now, and a
 | print then makes the PDF only and reports this.  Saved as {c:print_to}.
Printer Queue | The print queue that is used when Print To is Printer.  The
 | list is made by the system when the panel opens: System default first (the
 | queue that the system uses when none is named), then each queue by its
 | name; the default queue of the system is marked, and so is a queue that
 | does not accept jobs now.  You choose from the list with {k:Left},
 | {k:Right}, {k:Space} or {k:F2}; you never type a name.  When the system has no queue
 | the field says No printers found, which is not an error.  A queue that
 | was saved and is not in the list now is kept and shown with "(not
 | available now)", not dropped.  Saved as {c:print_queue}; the system default
 | is saved as nothing.
PDF Directory | The directory in which the PDF of File only and of PDF
 | viewer is made.  Empty, the default, is the directory of the document.  A
 | full path, or a path that starts with {c:~/} for your home directory; a
 | relative path is refused.  The panels of the Print menu then offer
 | {c:NAME.pdf} ({c:NAME-draft.pdf} for a Draft) in that directory; a name that you
 | type in the panel is kept.  The directory must exist and be writable when
 | it is used: Setup shows at the foot of the panel when it is not, and saves
 | it all the same, because it may be made later; the Print panel then uses
 | the directory of the document and reports this on the message line.  Saved as {c:pdf_dir}.
PDF Viewer | The program that shows the PDF when Print To (or the Printer
 | field of a Print panel) is PDF viewer.  It is the full path of the
 | program, and nothing else, so that no program of your {c:PATH} can take
 | its place: it starts with a slash, holds only letters, digits and the
 | characters . _ + - /, no space and no {c:..}.  A name such as {c:evince} or a
 | relative path such as {c:./viewer} is refused with the words "The PDF
 | viewer needs its full path, such as {c:/usr/bin/evince}".  The
 | program must be an executable file that other users cannot change: a file
 | that its group or others can write, a file of another user (root is
 | allowed), or a file in a directory that others can write (the sticky bit
 | does not make it right) is not safe and is refused, with the reason at the
 | foot.  An empty field, the default, means the first of evince, okular,
 | atril, zathura, qpdfview, mupdf, xpdf and epdfview that is in {c:/usr/bin},
 | {c:/usr/local/bin} or {c:/bin} (never in your {c:PATH} and never in the current
 | directory); when there is none, a print says "No PDF viewer found: set one
 | in Setup".  The line at the foot of the panel shows what was found, for
 | example "Found: /usr/bin/evince", or "Not an executable file", or "Not
 | safe".  A word processor (soffice, libreoffice, oowriter, oodraw, lowriter,
 | lodraw, abiword, wps, calligra, winword) is refused, because it would open
 | the PDF as a document to change and not to print; the names are a
 | courtesy, and a program with another name is not stopped by them.  The
 | program is run by its full path, with no shell, with the full path of the
 | PDF as its only argument, with your environment but for {c:LD_PRELOAD} and
 | {c:LD_AUDIT}, and is not waited for.  A {c:pdf_viewer} of an older settings file
 | that is a name or a relative path is not run: it is treated as not given,
 | and Setup reports this at the field.  A value in a settings file that other
 | users can write is ignored.  Saved as {c:pdf_viewer}.
Editor program | The path of the Editor, used as typed from now on.  The
 | default is the path that was found at start (see {n:str-find-editor}); a
 | path equal to it is saved as nothing.  {k:Keypad +} lists
 | the files of a directory.  Saved as the Editor program key of the
 file ({n:appcfg}).
Help File | The path of the help file, one file for the Editor and the
 | Document Manager in every version.  An empty field means no help.
 | {k:Keypad +} lists the files that end in {c:.hlp}.  The file is read
 | again when the panel is accepted.  Saved
 | as {c:help}.
Autosave | A choice, Yes or No: {k:Left} and {k:Right} change it.  Whether
 | the Editor saves a changed document by itself.  Saved as {c:autosave}; a
 | settings file may still have {c:yes} or {c:no} in any case of letters.
Autosave Interval (Seconds) | A number from 1 to 86400.  The seconds between
 | two autosaves.  It is read as a number, so that {c:90s} means {c:90}.  Saved
 | as {c:autosave_seconds}.
Window Rows | One line with two edit fields: X11 and Terminal.  {k:Tab} (or
 | {k:Right} at the end of the text) moves from the first to the second, and
 | {k:Shift-Tab} (or {k:Left} at the start) goes back.  Each is a whole
 | number of rows; a number below 20 is saved as 20 and a number above 200
 | as 200.  X11: the number of rows of the window of the X11 version; empty
 | means 50 rows, and the window is never taller than the screen.  The
 | Editor and the Document Manager open their window with this height, and
 | keep the height here by themselves when you change the height of the
 | window with the window manager ({n:appx11-size}).  Saved as {c:x11_rows};
 | 50 is saved as nothing.  Terminal: the most rows that the Editor of the
 | terminal version uses; empty means all the rows of the terminal, and a
 | terminal that has fewer rows is used as it is.  The program never changes
 | the size of your terminal window; the height that you gave
 | the terminal is kept here when the run ends ({n:appterm-size}).  Saved as
 | {c:terminal_rows}.
Terminal Characters | How the terminal version draws its screen.  The choices
 | are Automatic, ASCII, VT100 and UTF-8, in this order, and the default is
 | VT100: the lines of the VT100 set, with ASCII characters only, which looks
 | right in every terminal that speaks VT100.  Automatic: the program checks
 | at its start how to draw the screen: with UTF-8 when the terminal reads it,
 | else with the lines of the VT100 set, else with ASCII characters.  ASCII,
 | VT100 and UTF-8 decide without the check.  {k:Space} goes through the
 | choices and round from the last to the first; {k:Left} and {k:Right} stop
 | at the first and the last.  It is for the terminal version; the window
 | version is not concerned.  Saved as {c:terminal_charset}, with the value
 | that was chosen; a settings file with no line for it means VT100
 | ({n:appterm-charset}).
Undo Levels (0 to 500) | The number of operations that the Editor keeps
 | for Undo ({n:edit-b-undo}).  0 turns Undo off.  A number above 500 is
 | made 500.  The Editor uses it at the next start.  Saved as
 | {c:undo_levels}.
Backups Kept (0 to 99) | The number of numbered backups, such as
 | {c:.backup/report.bak01}, that a Save keeps of the file that it replaces
 | ({n:file-backup}).  The default is 3.  0 makes none.  A number above 99 is made 99.  The
 | line under the field shows how many whole copies are kept and that they
 | are in the directory .backup.  A lower number than before puts a question first, in a
 | box ({n:file-backup-option}); {k:N} keeps the old number and the rest of
 | Setup is saved.  Saved as {c:backups}; 3 is saved as nothing.  The old key
 | {c:backup} (yes or no) of the first versions is not read, because those
 | versions wrote it in every file; it is never saved.
.end
.h3 Choices and pages of Setup [str-setup-keys]
.ix Setup, keys
.ix Setup, pages
.ix PgUp, Setup
.ix Choice field, keys
The keys of Setup are those of the panels of the Editor.  A panel of several
pages: {k:PgUp} goes forward, {k:PgDn} goes backward, {k:Ins} accepts all the
pages and {k:Esc} cancels all of them.  A field with choices (Autosave, Print
To, Printer Queue, Paper for New Documents and Terminal Characters):
.table 13 47
Key | What it does
{k:Right} | The next choice; nothing at the last.
{k:Left} | The choice before; nothing at the first.
{k:Space} | The next choice, round to the first after the last.
{k:F2}, {k:Keypad +} | The list of the choices.
{k:Del} | The default.
.end
A letter does nothing in a field with choices.  {k:Enter}, {k:F2} and {k:Keypad +} never
open another panel in Setup: every page is part of the one panel.
.h3 The colors of the screen [str-colours]
.ix Colors, screen
.ix Colors panel
.ix Screen colors
.ix Contrast, warning
.ix Default colors
.ix Colors, reset
.ix Colors, recovery
The second page of Setup (reached with {k:PgUp} from the first page) sets the
foreground and the background color of every kind of text that the Editor
and the Document Manager show.  The colors are for the screen only, in the
Editor, the Document Manager and their panels; a printed page, the PDF and
the Preview are not changed by them, and the Viewer has its own colors.  A
screen that has no setting is blue, with yellow text.
.pic pictures/str-colors-panel.png 5 The page of the colors in Setup: the colors that the programs have until you change them.
.h4 The kinds of text
Each row of the page is a kind of text, with its foreground color, its
background color and a sample drawn in them.  These are the kinds and the
colors that the programs have until you change them.
.table 13 15 22
Kind of text | Colors, foreground on background | Where it is used
Normal text | yellow on blue | The text of a document, and the ground of the
 | | screen.
Bold text | white on blue | Bold text, headlines, the names of the fields of
 | | a panel when they are bold.
Italic text | light-green on blue | Text in italics.
Underlined text | yellow on blue | Underlined text, with the line.
Markers | light-cyan on blue | The labels of the markers in the text.
Dim text, ruler | light-grey on blue | The dots for spaces, the signs of tabs
 | | and line ends, the ruler and the rules.
Highlight | black on cyan | The area that Copy, Cut, Delete or Move acts on.
Print Error markers | light-red on blue | The markers that a failed Print
 | | adds to the text.
Keycaps | black on light-grey | A Keycap marker; the X11 version also draws a
 | | box round it.
Status line | black on light-grey | The first row of the Editor, and the
 | | title bar of a long text.
Message line | black on light-grey | The last row of the Editor and of the
 | | Document Manager.
Function key labels | black on light-grey | The two rows of key labels.
Menus | black on light-grey | The menu of the Editor and the bar of the spell
 | | check.
Panels | black on light-grey | Every box and panel, and the text in them.
Selected entry | white on black | The entry of a list, a menu or a panel on
 | | which the focus is, and the chosen entry of a menu bar.
Cursor | black on #ffb000 | The cursor of the X11 version (an amber block);
 | | the terminal gets this color for its own cursor where it can set it.
.end
A color of a kind is a part of it: bold text in a panel keeps the
color of the panel and gets the bold face, and dim text in a panel is
between the colors of the panel.  Bold, italic and underlined text keep
their faces and their lines whatever colors you choose.
.h4 Choosing a color
The page works with these keys.  The color of the entry is chosen as any
choice of Setup is.
.table 17 43
Key | What it does
{k:Up}, {k:Down}, {k:Enter} | Choose the kind of text; {k:Home} and
 | {k:End} go to the first and the last.
{k:Tab} | Switch between the foreground color and the background color of
 | the kind.
{k:Right} | The next color of the palette; nothing at the last.
{k:Left} | The color before; nothing at the first.
{k:Space} | The next color, round to the first after the last.
{k:F2}, {k:Keypad +} | Open the palette, a grid of colors, to choose one.
{k:Del} or {k:D} | Take the default color for this entry.
{k:R} | Take the default colors for every kind of this version, after the
 | question "Use the default colors for every kind of text?".
{k:V} | Show and change the colors of the other version, the terminal or
 | X11, whichever is not running.
{k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn} | Go to another page of Setup.
{k:Ins} | Keep the colors, and every other change of Setup, in the settings
 | file and close Setup.
{k:Esc} | Close Setup and take back every change, the colors too.
{k:F1} | Help.
.end
A color that you choose is in use at once.  {k:Esc} takes it back, and
{k:Ins} keeps it in the file {c:config} ({n:appcfg-key-colors}).  The Editor
reads the colors at start, so a change is seen in an Editor that is started
afterwards.
The palette is a grid of colors; {k:Left}, {k:Right}, {k:Up} and {k:Down}
move in it, {k:Enter} chooses the color that is marked and {k:Esc} closes
the palette and keeps the color as it was.  A line under the grid names the color.  What the grid holds
depends on the version.
.pic pictures/str-colors-palette.png 5 The palette of the X11 version, opened with F2 on the color of the entry.
.table 10 50
Version | The palette
X11 | The 16 colors of the VGA palette, 16 greys and 48 colors of 16 hues at
 | three strengths: 80 colors.  {k:T} prompts for a name or any color
 | {c:#rrggbb}.
Terminal | The 16 colors of the VGA palette.  Where the terminal shows 256
 | colors, the 240 others of its palette follow.  Where the terminal reports
 | that it shows any color, {k:T} prompts for a name or any color
 | {c:#rrggbb}.
.end
{k:T} (also {k:#}) in a terminal that shows 16 or 256 colors only gives the box
{c:This terminal shows 16 or 256 colors: choose one from the palette}; choose
the color in the grid.  A name that is not a color gives the box {c:That is not
a color: write a name such as light-cyan, or #rrggbb}, and the box prompts
again.
The 16 colors of the VGA palette have these names, which can also be written
in the file: black, blue, green, cyan, red, magenta, brown, light-grey,
dark-grey, light-blue, light-green, light-cyan, light-red, light-magenta,
yellow and white.  Capitals, spaces, hyphens and underscores do not
matter, so Light Grey is light-grey; gray is the same as grey, and the names
bright-green, bright-white and so on are the light colors.
.h4 What a terminal shows
The terminal version requests the colors of its text from the terminal, and it
paints the background of every cell, which clears the screen with the color
of normal text.  When the program ends, also when a signal stops it, the
colors and the cursor of the terminal are put back.  How many colors the
terminal shows is found in this order: the environment variable
{c:STRATA_COLORS} (the words none, 16, 256 or truecolor), the variable
COLORTERM (truecolor or 24bit), and the name of the terminal in {c:TERM}
(a name with 256color has 256 colors; a name with truecolor or direct has
all; dumb, vt100 and the other names that start with vt have none; the
rest have 16).  A color that the terminal cannot show is replaced by the
nearest that it can: of the 256, or of the 16.  A terminal that shows no
colors gets the text attributes of the old terminals, as the programs always
sent them: bold, dim, underline and reverse video, with cyan for markers;
Setup still lets you set the colors for another terminal or for X11.
.h4 Colors that are hard to read
The page warns, in the row under the list, when the text color of the kind
that is chosen is too near its background color: the contrast is less than
3 to 1.  The warning rejects nothing.
.h4 Getting the colors back
A screen that has become unreadable is mended in either of three ways.  Start
the Document Manager or the Editor with the option {c:--default-colors}, or
set the environment variable {c:STRATA_DEFAULT_COLORS} to 1: the program uses
the
colors that it is delivered with and ignores the color settings; the
Editor that the Document Manager starts does the same.  Or open Setup as
usual, press {k:PgUp} for the colors, then {k:R} and {k:Ins}.  Or take the lines of the colors out of the
file {c:config}.
.h3 Accepting the panel [str-setup-accept]
.ix Setup, accept
.ix Setup, validation
.ix Settings not saved
.ix Settings saved to
.ix Settings are in effect but could not be saved
{k:Ins} accepts the panel and {k:Esc} cancels it: nothing is changed.  A
value is checked when you leave its field, and all of them are checked when
{k:Ins} is pressed.  A wrong value never closes the panel: the focus goes to
the field that is wrong (or stays in it), the line at the foot shows the
reason in bold in place of the words about the field, and every other value
that you typed is kept.  Type the right value, or press {k:Del} for the
default, and go on.  {k:Esc} still throws the whole panel away.
.table 24 36
Check | Reason shown
The interval is not 1 to 86400 | The autosave interval is 1 to 86400 seconds
A Window Rows field is not empty or a number | The window rows are a whole
 | number, or empty
Undo Levels is not a number | Undo Levels is a whole number from 0 to 500
Backups Kept is not a number | Backups Kept is a whole number from 0 to 99
The PDF Directory is a relative path | The PDF Directory is a full path or
 | starts with {c:~/}
The PDF Viewer is not a full path | The PDF viewer needs its full
 | path, such as {c:/usr/bin/evince}
The PDF Viewer is not an executable file | The PDF viewer is not an
 | executable file
The PDF Viewer can be changed by others | The PDF viewer or its directory can
 | be changed by other users: it is not used
The PDF Viewer is a word processor | A word processor is not a PDF viewer
The Dictionary Language is not a code | The Dictionary Language is a code
 | such as en_US or de_DE
The Initial Working Directory does not exist | The initial working
 | directory does not exist
.end
Autosave, Print To, Printer Queue, Paper for New Documents and Terminal
Characters are choices: they cannot be wrong.  Backups Kept, when it is
lowered, still puts a question first, in a box, after {k:Ins}.
.pic pictures/str-setup-error.png 5 A refused value: the reason is in the line at the foot of the panel, and the focus stays in the field.
When all checks pass the new values are in effect at once, the help file is
read again, and the file is written.  The
footer then shows "Settings saved to PATH", with the full path of the
file.  If the file cannot be written the notice "Settings are in effect but
could not be saved" is shown instead: the values are used until the program
ends.  The file also holds the settings of the Editor (its Options panel);
Setup does not change them.
.pic pictures/str-setup-browse.png 5 The list of directories of Setup.
.note The settings of the Document Manager are saved only by Setup.  The
  directory and the document name that the panels remember last only for the
  run of the program.  The list of fonts is saved on its own (see
  {n:str-fonts}).
